0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n20050375551 aZeilinger, Martin,econtributor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no20140916751 aZemnukhova, Liliia,econtributor.1 aZylinska, Joanna,econtributor.2 a!Mediengruppe Bitnik,eContributor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/nb20140264762 a00Zhang,eContributor.2 aeeefff,eContributor.2 aFantastic little splash,eContributor.2 aFragmentin,eContributor.2 aLibrary Stack,edistributor.2 aMattin,eContributor.2 aMycological Twist,eContributor.2 aOmsk Social Club,eContributor.2 aPorpentine Charity Heartscape,eContributor.2 aPWR,eContributor.2 aTrust,eContributor.2 aTypical Organization,eContributor.2 aVOJD,eContributor.2 azzyw,eContributor.40zAvailable to Stanford-affiliated users.uhttps://www.librarystack.org/chimeras-inventory-of-synthetic-cognition/yLibrary StackxProvider: Library StackxpurchasedxeLoaderURLxls4zTo create lists or check out books that are not open access, users must create a Library Stack account.xls9970442uhttps://www.librarystack.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/07/1-Chimeras-CVR-480x720.pngzCover Image Preview  aeLoaderRecord xls4ffi2d13d863-e667-47fb-b05e-77fdb4ecdb5bs1aba9c3d-b526-4453-8453-78068c47f02908084nab a2201225ui in0000030072020250403135025.0m||||go|||||||||||cr mn|---uuuuu230226s2021||||xx || | |||||||   ||eng|d  als122313  aNYHLSbengerdacNYHLSdCstdUtOrBLW00aExiles and Exoduses 1a[Place of publication not identified] :bQuodlibet,c2021 1a[Place of publication not identified] :bUniversità Iuav di Venezia,c2021  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier0 aVesper ;v4  aArchived and cataloged by Library Stack  aUnrestricted online access  a"Two movements, perhaps antithetical, affect space. Individuals exclude themselves, exit (exilium, exsul, ex-solum), come out of their own land, withdraw into another circumstance, depose power from within, shun the power that withholds. Exile can be an individual choice, but it can also be a constraint that involves, cumulatively, a large number. At the same time, peoples, animals, and plants are in exodus, moving, fleeing, migrating, changing the design and the sense of territory and geographies. Three figures take shape from these movements: the space of the journey and the traces of the crossing, the destination or just the place of arrival, and finally the image of the house, of the city, or of the abandoned 'homeland'. These figures unite the two movements: certainly in exile the journey can be instantaneous; it can last as long as it takes to make a decision, to refuse, to write a text, to close a door. During the exodus, by contrast, the journey can prove to be the destination itself - in flight one may have to stop from necessity, by force, or by choice. Exiles and exoduses sediment themselves in history, sometimes finding space in the chronicle with novel accents. While the need for a gaze from the optimal distance is reiterated, the stones of other cities are silently laid, and the blending inheritance is renewed. The two movements perhaps tell us stories of new and repeating geneses."--cprovided by distributor  aCC BY-NC-ND10aArchitectural criticism.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8500658310aArchitecture.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8500661110aBorder security.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh200600750510aCity planning.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8502628210aClimatic changes.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8502703710aCosmology.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8503316910aEcology.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8504075210aGeopolitics.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8505419210aRefugees--Legal status, laws, etc.10aTerritory, National.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8513411017aArchitectural criticism.2fast0(OCoLC)fst008131