/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/e-flux-journal-issue-105-dragged-480x640.pngzCover Image Preview  aeLoaderRecord xls4ffi30c4ad56-2a32-4649-8d72-db462e282349s4787ed8c-b6fd-492c-a79c-749b5fbc5cc307842nab a2201081ui in0000030179820250403135035.0m||||go|||||||||||cr mn|---uuuuu191219s2019||||xx || | |||||||   ||eng|d  als24585  aNYHLSbengerdacNYHLSdCstdUtOrBLW1 aMunari, Bruno,eauthor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7904355614aThe Serving Library Annual 2019/20 1a[Place of publication not identified] :bCorraini Edizioni,c2019 1a[Place of publication not identified] :bRoma Publications,c2019 1a[Place of publication not identified] :bThe Serving Library,c2019  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier0 aBulletins of the Serving Library ;v15  aArchived and cataloged by Library Stack  aUnrestricted online access  a"The 2019/20 issue of The Serving Library Annual is entirely devoted to the late Italian designer, artist, inventor and polymath Bruno Munari. The core of the annual is the first English translation of Obvious Code, the 1971 collection of Munari's own writings, sketches and poems about his own work, published by arrangement with Corraini, who issued the book's anastatic edition in 2017. It includes iconic design objects such as the Abitacolo, ground-breaking artworks such as his 1952 series of hand-made projection slides, and little known rhymes about the art market, as well as an original piece from his "unreadable books" series. In the margins, dozens of artists, designers, writers and curators have been invited to annotate Munari's texts - with a sketch or a quotation, an in-depth analysis, a fragment of conversation, a free association - as a testament to the depth of the influence exerted on international art by an often under acknowledged pioneer, whose visual experiments were so iconic as to become a self-evident part of visual culture, an anonymous invention: an obvious code."--cprovided by distributor  aStandard Copyright10aArt and literature.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8500796210aArtxHistory.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8500748810aArtists' writings.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8500830610aComputational intelligence.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh9400465910aComputer art.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8502948210aGraphic arts.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8505647417aArt History.2fast0(OCoLC)fst0081539617aArtists' Writing.2fast0(OCoLC)fst0081770717aArts and Literature.2fast0(OCoLC)fst0081540017aComputation.2fast0(OCoLC)fst0087199517aComputer art.2fast0(OCoLC)fst008720330http://id.worldcat.org/fast/87203317aGraphic Design.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00946595 7aPeriodicals.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026139 7aPeriodicals.2fast0(OCoLC)fst014116410http://id.worldcat.org/fast/14116411 aAnceschi, Giovanni,econtributor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n820140271 aAndersen, Thom,econtributor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n950729971 aAntonelli, Paola,econtributor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no950563751 aAntunes, Leonor,econtributor.1 aBeal, Justin,econtributor.1 aBell, Eugenia,econtributor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no20010434251 aBertolotti-Bailey, Francesca,eeditor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no20180041921 aBertolotti-Bailey, Stuart,eeditor,econtributor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no20180040971 aBresson, Robert,econtributor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n500442881 aCasavecchia, Barbara,econtributor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/nr20030413021 aCondorelli, Céline,econtributor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/nb20130207671 aCosta, Valérie Da,econtributor.1 aCrasset, Matali,econtributor.1 aCrowe, Michael,econtributor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n